});

CSP (Content Security Policy) is an HTTP header that prevents XSS attacks by controlling which resources the browser is allowed to load.

In Node.js/Express:
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet.contentSecurityPolicy({
  directives: {
    defaultSrc: ["'self'"],
    scriptSrc: ["'self'", "https://cdn.example.com"],
    styleSrc: ["'self'", "'unsafe-inline'"],
    imgSrc: ["'self'", "data:", "https:"],
    connectSrc: ["'self'", "https://api.example.com"],
    fontSrc: ["'self'", "https://fonts.googleapis.com"],
    objectSrc: ["'none'"],
    upgradeInsecureRequests: []
  }
}));

This tells the browser: only load scripts from our domain and cdn.example.com, block inline scripts (prevents XSS), only connect to our API. Use the helmet package — it sets CSP and other security headers.

CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) allows a server to specify which origins can access its resources.

// Install
npm install cors

// Basic — allow all origins (development only)
const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors());

// Production — specific origins
app.use(cors({
  origin: ['https://myapp.com', 'https://admin.myapp.com'],
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  credentials: true, // Allow cookies
  maxAge: 86400 // Cache preflight for 24 hours
}));

// Per-route CORS
app.get('/api/public', cors(), (req, res) => { ... });

How it works: Browser sends OPTIONS preflight request → Server responds with allowed origins/methods → Browser proceeds or blocks. CORS is enforced by the browser, not the server.

CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) tricks a logged-in user into making unwanted requests. Prevention in Node.js:

1. CSRF Token (most common):
const csrf = require('csurf');
app.use(csrf({ cookie: true }));
app.get('/form', (req, res) => {
  res.render('form', { csrfToken: req.csrfToken() });
});
// In form: <input type='hidden' name='_csrf' value='<%= csrfToken %>'>

2. SameSite Cookie attribute:
res.cookie('session', token, { sameSite: 'strict', httpOnly: true, secure: true });

3. Double Submit Cookie: Send CSRF token in both cookie and header, server verifies they match.

4. Check Origin/Referer headers.

5. For APIs: Use Authorization header (Bearer tokens) instead of cookies — APIs using JWT in headers are naturally CSRF-proof since the attacker can't read the token.

