Server-Sent Events (SSE) is a technology where the server pushes updates to the client over a single HTTP connection. It's one-way: server → client only.

// Server (Node.js/Express)
app.get('/api/events', (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');

  // Send data every 2 seconds
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({ time: new Date(), message: 'Update!' })}\n\n`);
  }, 2000);

  req.on('close', () => clearInterval(interval));
});

// Client (Browser)
const source = new EventSource('/api/events');
source.onmessage = (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
  console.log('Received:', data);
};
source.onerror = () => console.error('Connection lost');

SSE vs WebSocket:
- SSE: One-way (server→client), HTTP-based, auto-reconnect, simpler
- WebSocket: Two-way, separate protocol, more complex

Use SSE for: Live feeds, notifications, stock prices, dashboards.

WebSockets provide full-duplex (two-way) communication between client and server over a single persistent connection.

// Server (using ws library)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
  console.log('Client connected');
  ws.on('message', (message) => {
    console.log('Received:', message);
    // Broadcast to all clients
    wss.clients.forEach(client => {
      if (client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
        client.send(JSON.stringify({ user: 'Siva', message }));
      }
    });
  });
  ws.on('close', () => console.log('Client disconnected'));
});

// Client (Browser)
const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8080');
ws.onopen = () => ws.send('Hello!');
ws.onmessage = (event) => console.log('Server says:', event.data);
ws.onclose = () => console.log('Disconnected');

How it works: HTTP upgrade handshake → persistent TCP connection → both sides can send data anytime.

Use cases: Chat apps, gaming, live collaboration, real-time trading. Use Socket.IO for easier API + auto-reconnect + fallback support.

Redis is an in-memory data store used as a cache, message broker, and session store. It stores data in RAM, making it extremely fast (sub-millisecond responses).

Data structures: Strings, Lists, Sets, Sorted Sets, Hashes, Streams.

When to use Redis:
1. Caching: Cache DB queries, API responses, session data
2. Session store: Store user sessions (express-session + connect-redis)
3. Rate limiting: Track request counts per IP/user
4. Real-time: Pub/Sub for live notifications, chat
5. Job queues: Bull/BullMQ uses Redis for background job processing
6. Leaderboards: Sorted sets for rankings
7. Distributed locks: Prevent race conditions across servers

// Basic usage with ioredis
const Redis = require('ioredis');
const redis = new Redis();

await redis.set('user:123', JSON.stringify(user));
await redis.expire('user:123', 3600); // TTL: 1 hour
const cached = JSON.parse(await redis.get('user:123'));

// Pub/Sub
redis.publish('notifications', JSON.stringify({ userId: 1, msg: 'New order' }));
redis.subscribe('notifications', (err) => {});
redis.on('message', (channel, message) => console.log(message));

Redis is NOT a replacement for MongoDB — it's a companion. Use MongoDB for persistent storage, Redis for speed-critical operations.
